# Line endings are a property of the COMMIT, not of the machine that made it.
#
# Without this file normalisation depends on each contributor's `core.autocrlf`.
# This machine has it on, so the index happens to be LF - but a clone with
# `autocrlf=false` commits CRLF, and part of this repo is PUBLIC. The web repo's
# `contract-in-sync` job curls `web/contract/v2/contract.schema.json` from
# GitHub and diffs it against its vendored copy BYTE FOR BYTE, so a CRLF commit
# breaks another track's build without touching a line of content.
#
# Brief 018 paid for this lesson once already: `models/baseline._fingerprint()`
# hashed raw bytes, so the same commit hashed differently under different
# autocrlf settings and verifying a historical version needed a search over 2^n
# line-ending conventions. The fix there was to remove the degree of freedom.
# This removes it at the source instead.
* text=auto

# Fetched over HTTP and compared byte for byte. Pinned in the working tree too,
# not just in the index, so what a contributor edits is what the gate diffs.
*.json text eol=lf
